# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Build output
dist/
build/

# Turborepo cache
.turbo/

# Generated Prisma clients (regenerate with `prisma generate`)
**/src/generated/

# Environment files — these hold database credentials
#
# The negation has to cover the same family the rule above it does. `.env.*`
# ignores `.env.ticketing.example` as readily as `.env.ticketing`, and
# `!.env.example` only rescues the exact name — so an example file for any
# variant is silently untracked, and the person who wrote it has no reason to
# look. Nothing depends on that today; the trap is that `git add` would appear
# to work and commit nothing.
#
# Same shape as the .dockerignore bug this PR fixes, which is why both are here:
# a rule matching one exact name where the repository has a family.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.*.example

# JWT signing keys — NEVER commit these
**/keys/
*.pem

# Helm's packaged dependencies: `helm dependency build` makes them from charts/ and the
# Chart.lock beside them, which IS committed.
charts/*/charts/

# Terraform (infra/terraform). The family, not one name of it:
#   - .terraform/ is the downloaded providers and module index;
#   - state holds every resource's attributes in plain text, secrets among them, in
#     terraform.tfstate, its backups (*.tfstate.backup) and numbered copies;
#   - variable files hold real values (addresses, account ids, emails), in .tfvars and
#     .tfvars.json alike; only the *.tfvars.example files are committed;
#   - a saved plan holds the same values as state;
#   - override files and CLI config are one machine's, and crash logs are dumps.
# The lock file, .terraform.lock.hcl, IS committed: one per root, pinning each provider
# to the version and checksums it was validated with.
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!*.tfvars.example
*.tfplan
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json
.terraformrc
terraform.rc
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Logs and OS noise
*.log
npm-debug.log*
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
